## ABSTRACT
Efficient hydrogen production via photocatalysis with high utilization efficiency of Pt cocatalyst is of great importance for sustainable development. In this work, we report an in situ auto-reduction strategy to encapsulate highly dispersed Pt clusters inside the cages of MIL-125- $\left.\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right.$ . The amino groups in MIL- $125\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ first react with formaldehyde to form reducing groups (i.e., $-N H-C H_{2}O H)$ , which can in situ auto-reduce the confined $\mathsf{P t}^{2+}$ ions to ultrasmall Pt clusters within the cavities. With optimized Pt content, photocatalytic ${\sf H}_{2}$ production over the obtained $P t(1.5)/N//L-125-N H-C H_{2}O H$ catalyst with $1.43\mathrm{\:wt.\%}$ Pt loading achieved as high as $4,496.4\upmu\mathrm{mol}\cdot\mathsf{g}^{-1}\cdot\mathsf{h}^{-1}$ under visible light $(\lambda>420\mathsf{n m})$ ) due to the facilitated transfer and separation of the photo-induced charger carriers arising from the synergetic effects between highly dispersed Pt clusters and MIL-125-NH-CH2OH framework. This in situ auto-reduction strategy may be extended to encapsulate various kinds of metal or alloy clusters/nanoparticles within amino-functioned metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) with superior properties and excellent performance.
## KEYWORDS
Pt clusters, MIL-125-NH2, in situ auto-reduction method, photocatalytic ${\sf H}_{2}$ production, visible light
## Introduction
Pt nanocrystals have been actively investigated in various catalytic fields due to their high catalytic activities and excellent physicochemical properties [1, 2]. However, owing to the high surface area to volume ratio and high surface energy, Pt clusters usually tend to grow or aggregate during the catalytic process which would lead to decayed performance. A promising strategy to promote the stability and maintain activity of Pt clusters is using porous materials as support to hinder their growth and aggregation by confining them [3]. In the last decades, porous metal-organic frameworks (MOFs)-based materials have been widely investigated in catalytic fields, including photo-, electro-, and thermo-catalysis due to their superior properties including large surface areas, high porosity, uniform but tunable cavities, and versatile composition as compared to traditional microand meso-porous materials [4–8]. Especially, MOFs supported metal nanoparticles, clusters or single atoms have been extensively demonstrated to exhibit better catalytic performance benefiting from the synergetic effects between MOFs and incorporated active phases [9–14]. Additionally, due to the superior nanoconfinement and electronic effects from MOF networks, the aggregation and leaching of metal clusters during the catalytic reactions will also be greatly minimized as compared to other supports (e.g., zeolites and mesoporous silica). Therefore, various kinds of $\mathrm{Pt}/\$ MOFs composites have been designed and prepared with the aims to improve the catalytic activity and stability [15].
The $\mathrm{Pt/MOFs}$ nanocomposites can be prepared either by using MOFs as the templates to support Pt clusters/nanoparticles or by encapsulating pre-synthesized Pt nanoparticles in MOFs. The mostly widely adopted strategy is using MOFs as template for the incorporation of metal clusters/nanoparticles, for which a variety of methodologies have been developed, such as traditional solution impregnation [16–19], arc plasma deposition method [20], and photoreduction deposition method [21, 22]. In the traditional solution impregnation method, a metal precursor (e.g., $\mathrm{K_{2}P t C l_{4}}$ and $\mathrm{H_{2}P t C l_{6}}\mathrm{\cdot}$ ) solution is first introduced into the porous framework of MOFs, then the embedded metal ion species are reduced to metallic species by the introduction of reducing agents, such as $\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}$ , $\mathrm{H}_{2},$ and ascorbic acid [23]. Whereas, the post-reduction process usually leads to the formation of $\mathrm{Pt}$ nanoparticles anchored on the external surface of MOFs [19]. To increase the Pt loading amount and reduce the particle size in the cavities of MOFs, a double-solvent approach has been developed by $\mathrm{{Xu}}$ et al. for the introduction of a small amount of $\mathrm{H_{2}P t C l_{6}}$ aqueous solution within MOFs followed by the reduction of metal species with flowing $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ gas [24]. Even though the double-solvent approach using $n$ -hexane as a hydrophobic solvent is effective in driving the $\mathrm{Pt}$ precursor solution into MOFs, additive $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ reducing process at relatively high temperature (e.g., $180{-}250~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ is usually needed to form metal nanoparticles [25, 26]. Pt nanoparticles encapsulated into the MOFs can also be obtained by building MOFs around pre-formed Pt nanoparticles [27–34], or pre-anchored Pt precursors on organic linkers [35–37]; however, additional protective agents such as surfactants, capping agents, or expensive linkers are usually needed. The difficulty in the complete removal of stabilizing agents may have a negative effect on the catalytic performance of Pt nanoparticles. Pt clusters or nanoparticles encapsulated MOFs can be obtained by in situ one-step synthesis via directly mixing Pt precursor (e.g., H2PtCl6) and MOF precursors in N,N-Dimethylformamide (DMF) [38, 39], whereas Pt nanoparticles with big sizes are usually obtained. Despite great achievements have been achieved in the synthesis of MOF supported Pt clusters/nanoparticles, it is still desirable to develop simple strategies to obtain uniformly distributed and controllable $\mathrm{Pt}$ clusters-encapsulated MOFs.
In this work, amino-functionalized Ti(IV)-based MOF (i.e., MIL- $125\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ ) was used as the supporting matrix for encapsulating Pt clusters through a simple in situ auto-reduction method. The amino groups in MIL- $125\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ first react with formaldehyde to form the reducing groups (i.e., MIL-125-NH$\mathrm{CH_{2}O H})$ ), on which $\mathrm{Pt}^{2+}$ ions are in situ auto-reduced to metallic Pt clusters in the absence of any additional reducing agent [40]. The obtained $\mathrm{Pt}(1.5)/\mathrm{MIL}\mathrm{-}125\mathrm{-}\mathrm{NH}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{OH}$ hybrid with uniform dispersion of $\mathrm{Pt}$ clusters $(1{-}2~\mathrm{nm})$ exhibited excellent activity toward photocatalytic $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ production under visible light irradiation $\lambda>420~\mathrm{nm}$ ). The in situ auto-reduction strategy in this work is applicable to synthesizing other kinds of aminofunctionalized MOF-supported metal or alloy clusters.
## 2 Experimental
## 2.1 Synthesis of Pt/MIL-125-NH-CH2OH
The general synthetic procedure of $\mathrm{Pt/MIL-}125\mathrm{-NH\mathrm{-}C H_{2}O H}$ composites is shown in Scheme 1. First, MIL-125-NH2 was prepared via a solvothermal method according to our previous literature using 2-aminoterephtalic acid as organic linker and titanium tetraisopropoxide as titanium precursor in a DMF/MeOH solution [41]. To introduce the reducing groups onto the organic frameworks in MIL- $125\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ , $\boldsymbol{1.0\mathrm{~g~}}$ MIL-125-NH2 was added into a $50~\mathrm{mL}$ mixture of formaldehyde, ethanol, and water (5:20:25, v/v/v) and stirred at $40~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $120~\mathrm{min}$ . The solid was separated by centrifugation, washed with ethanol several times, dried under vacuum at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $12\mathrm{{h}}.$ and named as MIL-125-NH- $\mathrm{CH_{2}O H}$ .
In order to drive the Pt precursor into cavities of MIL$125\mathrm{-NH\mathrm{-CH_{2}O H_{2}}}$ a double-solvent method using n-hexane as a hydrophobic solvent was adopted [42]. Typically, $_{0.2\mathrm{~g~}}$ MIL-125-NH- $\mathrm{CH_{2}O H}$ was first homogeneously dispersed in $20~\mathrm{mL}$ anhydrous n-hexane under continuous stirring. Then a certain amount of $\mathrm{(NH_{4})_{2}P t C l_{4}}$ aqueous solution with controlled $\mathrm{Pt}^{2+}$ content was added dropwise to this solution under continuous stirring. After the mixture had been stirred for $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ at $40\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ the supernatant was decanted by rubber pipette. After drying under vacuum for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ the obtained solid samples were named as $\mathrm{Pt}(x)/\mathrm{MIL}\mathrm{-}125\mathrm{-}\mathrm{NH}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{OH}$ $\langle x=0.5;$ , 1.0, 1.5, or 2.0) depending on the added Pt content, in which 0.5, 1.0, 1.5, and 2.0 is the added weight percent of Pt in wt. $\%$ to $\mathrm{MIL-}125{\mathrm{-NH-CH}}_{2}\mathrm{OH}$ . A control sample was prepared using MIL- $125\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ as support for the incorporation of 1.5 wt $\%$ Pt via the same procedure as that to prepare $\mathrm{Pt}(1.5)/\mathrm{MIL}\mathrm{-}125\mathrm{-}\mathrm{NH}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{OH},$ and the final sample was named as $\mathrm{Pt^{2+}}(1.5)/\mathrm{MIL}\mathrm{-}125\mathrm{-NH_{2}}$ . Then $0.15\mathrm{\g\Pt^{2+}(1.5)/M I L\mathrm{-}125\mathrm{-}N H_{2}}$ was dispersed in 5 mL $\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}$ aqueous solution $(0.6~\mathrm{mol}\cdot\mathrm{L}^{-1})$ , stirred for $5~\mathrm{min}$ , and then washed with deionized water for
3 times. After vacuum drying at $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $12\mathrm{{h}}.$ , the final product was named as $\mathrm{Pt}(1.5)/\mathrm{MIL}\mathrm{-}125\mathrm{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{NaBH}_{4}$ .
## 2.2 Characterization
Powder X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns were recorded using a Bruker D8 Advance X-ray diffractometer with $\mathrm{Cu}\ K\alpha$ radiation $(\lambda=1.541\ \mathring\mathrm{\mathrm{\AA}})$ ). The size and morphology of the as-prepared samples was checked by transmission electron microscopy (TEM, Tecnai G2 F20 S-TWIN) and field emission scanning electron microscopy (FESEM, Hitachi SU8010). X-ray photoelectron spectrometer (XPS) measurements were tested on PHI Quantera SXM, ULVAC-PHI Inc., Japan with C 1s as reference. Inductivity coupled plasma-atomic emission spectrometry (ICP-AES, Agilent $7500c e)$ was used to determine the exact Pt contents in these samples. Fourier-transform infrared (FT-IR) spectra were obtained on a Nicolet 6700 Fourier spectrometer. Solid state $^{13}\mathrm{C}$ nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR) spectra were collected with a Bruker Avance III $400~\mathrm{{M}}$ NMR spectrometer. $^{1}\mathrm{H}$ NMR was tested on Varian UnityPlus 400 after the MOFs were digested in DMSO d6 solvent. The photocurrent–time test was performed by dropping the mixture of Pt/MIL-125-NH-CH2OH composite and ethanol solvent onto a $1\times1$ conductive glass and connecting it to electrochemical workstation (CHI660D, Shanghai, China) on a three-electrode system. Steady-state photoluminescence (PL) spectra were obtained by fluoromax-4 spectrophotometer. Time-resolved PL spectra (excitation at $400~\mathrm{nm}$ and emission at $455\ \mathrm{nm}$ ) were obtained from Edinburgh Instruments FLS1000/FS5 with a picosecond pulse laser as the excitation source. The data were fitted and analyzed by a built-in software supplied by Edinburgh Instruments. Electron spin resonance (ESR) spectra were obtained on Bruker-A300-10/12 electron paramagnetic resonance spectrometer in the mixture of photocatalyst and MeCN/triethanolamine (TEOA) $\mathrm{{^{\prime}H_{2}O}}$ (8/1/1) under visible light irradiation for $90\:s.$ .
## 2.3 Photocatalytic test
In a typical experiment, the photocatalyst sample $\left(5.0\:\mathrm{mg}\right)$ was homogeneously dispersed in a mixture of acetonitrile $\mathrm{\CH_{3}C N,}$ $16~\mathrm{mL}$ ), TEOA $(2~\mathrm{mL})$ , and deionized water $(2{\mathrm{~mL}})$ ) under ultrasonication for $5~\mathrm{min}$ . Afterward, the yellow suspension in a photoreactor (total volume approximately $30~\mathrm{mL}$ ) was degassed by bubbling $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ for approximately $30\ \mathrm{min}$ during stirring before the irradiation by visible light with a $300~\mathrm{W}$ xenon lamp using a $420\mathrm{nm}$ ultraviolet (UV) cutoff filter. After irradiation for $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ , the evolved $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ was determined using a gas injector by gas chromatography (Shimadzu GC-2014, molecular sieve column, $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ carrier, and thermal conductivity detector (TCD)) and was quantified by a calibration plot to the internal hydrogen standard.
## 3 Results and discussion
As compared with pristine MIL- $125\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ , the $\mathrm{C-N}$ linkage in MIL-125-NH- $\mathrm{CH_{2}O H}$ and $\mathrm{Pt}(x)/\mathrm{MIL}\mathrm{-}125\mathrm{-}\mathrm{NH}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{OH}$ samples is shifted slightly to higher binding energy, which can be attributed to the formation of $\mathrm{-NH-CH_{2}O H}$ groups. The exact loading content of $\mathrm{Pt}$ in these samples was determined to be 0.49 wt. $\%$ , $0.92\ \mathrm{wt.\%}$ , $1.43\mathrm{\wt.\%}$ , $1.89\mathrm{~wt.\%}$ , and $1.58~\mathrm{wt.\%}$ for $\mathrm{Pt}(0.5)/\mathrm{MIL}\mathrm{-}125\mathrm{-}\mathrm{NH}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{OH}$ , $\mathrm{Pt}(1.0)/\mathrm{MIL}\mathrm{-}125\mathrm{-}\mathrm{NH}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{OH},$ $\mathrm{Pt}(1.5)/\mathrm{MIL}\mathrm{-}125\mathrm{-}\mathrm{NH}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{OH} $ , $\mathrm{Pt}(2.0)/\mathrm{MIL}\mathrm{-}125\mathrm{-}\mathrm{NH}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{OH}.$ and $\mathrm{Pt}(1.5)/\mathrm{MIL}\mathrm{-}125\mathrm{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{NaBH}_{4}$ , respectively, according to the ICP-AES results, which is similar to the added content.
The UV–vis diffuse reflectance spectra for MIL-125-NH$\mathrm{CH_{2}O H}$ and $\mathrm{Pt}(x)/\mathrm{MIL}\mathrm{-}125\mathrm{-}\mathrm{NH}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{OH}$ well inherit the feature of MIL-125-NH2. All the samples show similar absorption in the region of approximately $300{-}450~\mathrm{nm}$ , indicating their similar light absorption and band gap with ca. $2.76\mathrm{eV}$ (Fig. 4(a)). The Pt loading does not obviously enhance the absorption ability, in agreement with their similar yellow color (Fig. S7 in the ESM). However, the color of $\mathrm{Pt}(1.5)/\mathrm{MIL}-125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{NaBH}_{4}$ is obviously darker than those obtained via in situ auto-reduction method, which can be attributed to the existence of abundant $\mathrm{Pt}$ nanoparticles on the external surface of MIL- $125\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ in $\mathrm{Pt}(1.5)/\mathrm{MIL}\mathrm{-}125\mathrm{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{NaBH}.$ 4. PL emission spectroscopy is a useful tool to verify the photoexcited charge transfer and recombination. As shown in Fig. 4(b), the PL of MIL- $125\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ is slightly weakened when the reducing groups are grafted on the MOFs. When the Pt clusters are supported on MIL-125- NH- ${\mathrm{CH}}_{2}{\mathrm{OH}}$ , there are only slight decreases in the PL of $\mathrm{Pt}(1.0)/\mathrm{MIL}\mathrm{-}125\mathrm{-}\mathrm{NH}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{OH}$ and $\mathrm{Pt}(2.0)/\mathrm{MIL}\mathrm{-}125\mathrm{-}\mathrm{NH}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{OH}.$ while obvious suppression is observed in the PL of Pt(1.5)/MIL125-NH- $\mathrm{CH_{2}O H}$ which can be attributed to the well-dispersion of Pt clusters in $\mathrm{Pt}(1.5)/\mathrm{MIL}\mathrm{-}125\mathrm{-}\mathrm{NH}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{OH}$ (Fig. 4(b)). The time-resolved PL spectroscopy was further examined at $455~\mathrm{nm}$ emission $(\lambda_{\mathrm{{ex}}}=400~\mathrm{{nm})}$ for MIL-125-NH-CH2OH and $\mathrm{Pt}(1.5)/\mathrm{MIL}\mathrm{-}125\mathrm{-}\mathrm{NH}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{OH}$ . As shown in Fig. 4(c), Pt(1.5)/ MIL-125-NH- $\mathrm{CH_{2}O H}$ has a longer fluorescence lifetime (i.e., 4.15 ns) than pure MIL-125-NH- $\mathrm{CH_{2}O H}$ (i.e., 2.76 ns), confirming that $\mathrm{Pt}(1.5)/\mathrm{MIL}\mathrm{-}125\mathrm{-}\mathrm{NH}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{OH}$ has a stronger electron–hole migration ability. Both the steady-state PL spectroscopy (Fig. 4(b)) and the PL lifetime (Fig. 4(c)) suggest that the photoexcited charge recombination in MIL-125-NH$\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{OH}$ can be obviously suppressed by the introduction of Pt clusters due to the fast photogenerated electron via the Pt clusters. The enhanced charge-separation efficiency for $\mathrm{Pt}(x)/\mathrm{MIL}\mathrm{-}125\mathrm{-}\mathrm{NH}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{OH}$ as compared to the pristine MIL-125-NH- $\mathrm{CH_{2}O H}$ was further demonstrated by the photocurrent results (Fig. 4(d)), revealing that the incorporation of Pt clusters in the cavities of MIL-125-NH- $\mathrm{CH_{2}O H}$ favors the photogenerated electron–hole separation. Especially, the $\mathrm{Pt}(1.5)/$ MIL-125-NH- $\mathrm{CH_{2}O H}$ exhibits much higher photocurrent than pristine MIL-125-NH- $\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{OH}$ and other $\mathrm{Pt}(x)/\mathrm{ML}-$ 125-NH- $\mathrm{CH_{2}O H}$ samples under the visible light irradiation, suggesting the efficient charge transfer from MIL-125-NH$\mathrm{CH_{2}O H}$ to Pt clusters in $\mathrm{Pt}(1.5)/\mathrm{MIL}\mathrm{-}125\mathrm{-}\mathrm{NH}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{OH}.$ As shown in the electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) (Fig. 4(e)), $\mathrm{Pt}(1.5)/\mathrm{MIL}\mathrm{-}125\mathrm{-}\mathrm{NH}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{OH}$ presents a smaller radius than other samples, indicating its lower charge-transfer resistance. These results indicate that the electron–hole separation efficiency of $\mathrm{MIL-125–NH-CH_{2}O H}$ is effectively enhanced by incorporating Pt clusters. The photocatalytic $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ production of $\mathrm{Pt}(x)/\mathrm{MIL}\mathrm{-}125\mathrm{-}\mathrm{NH}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{OH}$ samples with different Pt loading content has been conducted with TEOA as sacrificial agent in $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CN}$ and water under visible-light irradiation (Fig. 4(f)). Compared to the poor activity of pristine MIL-125-NH2 $76~{\upmu\mathrm{mol}}{\cdot\mathrm{g}^{-1}}{\cdot\mathrm{h}^{-1}})$ and MIL-125-NH- $\mathrm{CH_{2}O H}$ $(144~\upmu\mathrm{mol\cdotg^{-1}\cdot h^{-1}})$ , the $\mathrm{Pt}(x)/\mathrm{MIL}\mathrm{-}125\mathrm{-}\mathrm{NH}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{OH}$ samples exhibit obviously higher photocatalytic $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ production rate, revealing the critical of Pt clusters for the effective charge spatial separation between $\mathrm{MIL-125–NH-CH_{2}O H}$ and Pt. Strikingly, the $\mathrm{Pt}(1.5)/\mathrm{MIL}\mathrm{-}125\mathrm{-}\mathrm{NH}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{OH}$ with $1.43\ \mathrm{wt.\%}$ Pt loading exhibits the highest activity $(4,496.4\ \upmu\mathrm{molcdotg^{-1}\cdot h^{-1}})$ , about 31 times higher than that of the parent MIL-125-NH- $.{\mathrm{CH}}_{2}{\mathrm{OH}}$ . With further increasing the $\mathrm{Pt}$ loading content to $1.89\ \mathrm{wt.\%}$ for $\mathrm{Pt}(2.0)/\mathrm{MIL}\mathrm{-}125\mathrm{-}\mathrm{NH}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{OH}.$ , the photocatalytic $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ production rate slightly reduces to $4{,}271{.}6\ \upmu\mathrm{mol{\cdot}g^{-1}{\cdot}h^{-1}}$ because of the shielding effect of the aggregated $\mathrm{Pt}$ clusters on the outer surface toward the incident light, which hampers photons to reach MIL-125-NH- $\mathrm{CH_{2}O H}$ particles to some extent (Fig. 2(h)). As compared with $\mathrm{Pt}(1.5)/\mathrm{MIL}\mathrm{-}125\mathrm{-}\mathrm{NH}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{OH}$ with $1.43~\mathrm{wt.\%}$ $\mathrm{Pt},\mathrm{Pt}(1.5)/\mathrm{MIL}\mathrm{-}125\mathrm{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{NaBH}_{4}$ with $1.58~\mathrm{wt.\%}$ Pt exhibits a bit higher activity $(5,541.1\upmu\mathrm{molcdotg^{-1}\cdot h^{-1}})$ , which can be attributed to the slightly higher Pt content and better light response ability (Fig. S8 in the ESM). However, the existence of Pt nanoparticles on the external surface of MIL- $125\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ in $\mathrm{Pt}(1.5)/\mathrm{MIL}-125-$ $\mathrm{NH}_{2}{-}\mathrm{NaBH}_{4}$ sample may result in the fall off of Pt nanoparticles during catalytic test and its more complex synthesis procedure would also increase the cost.
## 4 Conclusions
In this work, we have demonstrated a general and effective in situ auto-reduction strategy to encapsulate $\mathrm{Pt}$ clusters in $\mathrm{MIL}{-}125{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ . The reducing groups $\left(-\mathrm{NH-CH_{2}O H}\right)$ formed via the reaction between amino groups and formaldehyde can in situ auto-reduce the confined $\mathrm{Pt}^{2+}$ ions to $\mathrm{Pt}$ clusters with high dispersion and ultrasmall size. The resultant $\mathrm{Pt}(x)/\mathrm{ML}.$ 125-NH- $\mathrm{CH_{2}O H}$ catalysts exhibit high catalytic activity in the photocatalytic hydrogenation production under visible light $\mathrm{(>420~nm)}$ ). With optimized Pt content, Pt(1.5)/MIL-125-NH$\mathrm{CH_{2}O H}$ catalyst with $1.43\ \mathrm{\wt.\%}$ Pt loading could exhibit $4{,}496{.}4\ \upmu\mathrm{mol{\cdot}g^{-1}{\cdot}h^{-1}}$ in photocatalytic $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ production. It is demonstrated that the facilitated transfer and separation of the photo-induced charger carriers arising from the synergetic effects between highly dispersed $\mathrm{Pt}$ clusters and MOFs contribute to the excellent activity. Considering the wide use of aminofunctionalized organic linkers in the synthesis of MOFs, we speculate that the in situ auto-reduction strategy can be extended to the synthesis of other amino-functionalized MOFs supported metal other noble metal or alloy (e.g., Pd, Ag, Au, and PtNi) clusters or nanoparticles for advanced catalytic applications. In our laboratory, such possibilities are currently under investigation.
